# Stable device names for the kobold MCU boards.
#
# Install:  sudo ./scripts/setup-udev.sh
#
# WHY THIS MATTERS: /dev/ttyUSB0 and /dev/ttyUSB1 swap between reboots depending
# on enumeration order. The failure mode is sending motor commands to the
# ultrasonic board, which is exactly as bad as it sounds.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THIS ROBOT: matched on SERIAL, which was reprogrammed into each board
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Both ESP32 DevKit V1 boards ship with a CP2102 bridge whose serial is left at
# the factory default -- read off the hardware 2026-08-04, both said "0001":
#
#     idVendor 10c4   idProduct ea60   serial "0001"   (on BOTH)
#
# Nothing electrically unique to match on, so the first version of this file
# matched on physical port. That worked, but it meant the ROLE FOLLOWED THE
# SOCKET: swapping two USB cables silently swapped drive and sense.
#
# Fixed properly by rewriting the CP2102 EEPROM serial on each board:
#
#     git clone https://github.com/DiUS/cp210x-cfg && cd cp210x-cfg && make
#     sudo ./cp210x-cfg -l                       # find bus/dev
#     sudo ./cp210x-cfg -d <bus>.<dev> -S KOBOLD-DRIVE
#     sudo ./cp210x-cfg -d <bus>.<dev> -S KOBOLD-SENSE
#
# Note -d wants n.n despite the help text saying bus:dev, and the device
# re-enumerates mid-write, so the tool reports "No such device" read errors
# after a SUCCESSFUL write. Verify with `cp210x-cfg -l` afterwards, not by
# trusting the exit code.
#
# Identity now follows the BOARD into any socket, which is what you want on a
# machine that gets taken apart. Label the boards to match their serials.
#
# Backstopped in software regardless: the firmware announces its board id in
# the version frame at boot, and kobold_bridge refuses to talk to a board whose
# id is not the one that port is meant to hold.
#
# Common bridge chips:
#   1a86:7523  CH340          (most DevKit V1 clones)
#   1a86:55d4  CH9102 / CH343 (many USB-C boards)
#   10c4:ea60  CP2102         (Silicon Labs; serial often left at "0001")

# ---- drive board: motors, encoders, IMU, cliff reflex ----
SUBSYSTEM=="tty", ATTRS{idVendor}=="10c4", ATTRS{idProduct}=="ea60", ATTRS{serial}=="KOBOLD-DRIVE", SYMLINK+="robot-drive", MODE="0660", GROUP="dialout"

# ---- sense board: ultrasonic, IR, buzzer, OLED, safety line ----
SUBSYSTEM=="tty", ATTRS{idVendor}=="10c4", ATTRS{idProduct}=="ea60", ATTRS{serial}=="KOBOLD-SENSE", SYMLINK+="robot-sense", MODE="0660", GROUP="dialout"

# ---- head board (Phase 5+): pan/tilt servos, PIR, second OLED ----
# The spare board is still on the factory "0001". Give it a serial before
# fitting it, or it will be indistinguishable from any other stock CP2102.
#SUBSYSTEM=="tty", ATTRS{idVendor}=="10c4", ATTRS{idProduct}=="ea60", ATTRS{serial}=="KOBOLD-HEAD", SYMLINK+="robot-head", MODE="0660", GROUP="dialout"

# ---- lidar (Phase 5, if ever fitted) ----
# DELIBERATELY NOT a bare 10c4:ea60 match. An earlier revision of this file had
# exactly that, and because these ESP32 boards use the same CP2102 bridge it
# grabbed them both: /dev/robot-lidar pointed at an ESP32, and the second board
# silently overwrote the first one's symlink. Match on that device's own serial.
#SUBSYSTEM=="tty", ATTRS{idVendor}=="10c4", ATTRS{idProduct}=="ea60", ATTRS{serial}=="<lidar serial>", SYMLINK+="robot-lidar", MODE="0660", GROUP="dialout"

# ---- fallback: boards with no distinguishing serial ----
# If you ever run identical boards you cannot reprogram, match on physical port
# and always use the same socket. Find paths with:
#   for d in /dev/ttyUSB*; do echo "$d $(udevadm info -q path -n $d)"; done
#SUBSYSTEM=="tty", ATTRS{idVendor}=="10c4", ATTRS{idProduct}=="ea60", KERNELS=="3-1.1", SYMLINK+="robot-drive", MODE="0660", GROUP="dialout"
#SUBSYSTEM=="tty", ATTRS{idVendor}=="10c4", ATTRS{idProduct}=="ea60", KERNELS=="3-1.2", SYMLINK+="robot-sense", MODE="0660", GROUP="dialout"
